  "live_records": {
    "q-213448": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213448",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14272954/assets/2090775d8190464698e28099faf2cb740a928cbf3f9abdc958aa58c09eeeb31f.png)\n\nA binary relation is represented by the digraph shown above. Select the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince the digraph has arrows\n{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}\nbut has no arrow\n{{field-3}},\nthe relation is \n{{field-4}}.",
      "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ then the arrow $x\\to z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nExamining the diagram, we conclude the following:\n\nSince the digraph has arrows $1\\to 3$ and $3\\to 1$ but has no arrow $1\\to 1,$ the relation is $\\text{not transitive}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
